\subsection{When do ratios of moments suffice?}\label{sec:conc}

The ratio of means replaces $\nu=\E[x/\|x\|]$ by $\phi=\E[x]/\sqrt\kappa$, with $\kappa=\E\|x\|^2$. The moment-ratio variance of Baumann et al.\ \cite[Eq.~(9)]{pvlm2024}, $\operatorname{Var}(x_i^\top x_j)/(\kappa_i\kappa_j)$, has the same structure: since $\E[(x_i^\top x_j)^2]=\tr(\E[x_ix_i^\top]\E[x_jx_j^\top])$ for independent inputs, it equals $\tr(\tilde G_i\tilde G_j)-(\phi_i^\top\phi_j)^2$ with $\tilde G=\E[xx^\top]/\kappa$, the exact variance with $(\nu,G)$ replaced by $(\phi,\tilde G)$. Both replacements are accurate when the squared norm concentrates around its mean.

\begin{proposition}[Concentration of the norm]\label{prop:conc}
Let $x\ne0$ almost surely with $\E\|x\|^4<\infty$, and let $\mathrm{CV}=\operatorname{sd}(\|x\|^2)/\kappa$. Then
\[
\|\nu-\phi\|\le\mathrm{CV},\qquad \|G-\tilde G\|_*\le\mathrm{CV},
\]
where $\|\cdot\|_*$ is the nuclear norm. For independent inputs, $|\nu_i^\top\nu_j-\phi_i^\top\phi_j|\le\mathrm{CV}_i+\mathrm{CV}_j$, and the moment-ratio variance differs from the exact variance by at most $3(\mathrm{CV}_i+\mathrm{CV}_j)$. For a mixture, the per-component ratio $\phi^{\mathrm{c}}=\sum_c r_c\phi_c$ satisfies $\|\nu-\phi^{\mathrm{c}}\|\le\sum_c r_c\mathrm{CV}_c$. For $x\sim\N(m,S)$,
\[
\mathrm{CV}^2=\frac{2\tr S^2+4m^\top Sm}{(\|m\|^2+\tr S)^2},
\]
which equals $2/D_{\mathrm{eff}}$ with $D_{\mathrm{eff}}=(\tr S)^2/\tr S^2$ when $m=0$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Write $r=\|x\|$. Then $\nu-\phi=\E[x(r^{-1}-\kappa^{-1/2})]$, so $\|\nu-\phi\|\le\E|1-r/\sqrt\kappa|=\E\bigl[|\kappa-r^2|/(\sqrt\kappa(\sqrt\kappa+r))\bigr]\le\E|\kappa-r^2|/\kappa\le\mathrm{CV}$. Likewise $G-\tilde G=\E[xx^\top(r^{-2}-\kappa^{-1})]$, and each rank-one term has nuclear norm $|1-r^2/\kappa|$. For the products, $\|\nu\|,\|\phi\|\le1$ and $\|G\|_2,\|\tilde G\|_2\le1$ (both are PSD with unit trace), so $|\tr(G_iG_j)-\tr(\tilde G_i\tilde G_j)|\le\|G_i-\tilde G_i\|_*+\|G_j-\tilde G_j\|_*$ and $|\mu^2-f^2|\le2|\mu-f|$. The mixture bound follows from the triangle inequality, and the Gaussian formula from $\operatorname{Var}(\|x\|^2)=2\tr S^2+4m^\top Sm$.
\end{proof}

The bound is a sufficient condition. It explains two observations below. A single Gaussian in high effective dimension has small CV, so all moment-based estimators agree. A mixture whose components have different norms has a large CV as a whole even when each component is concentrated, so the global ratio can fail while the per-component ratio does not.

