\paragraph{Concentration diagnostic.} Figure~\ref{fig:cv} compares, for each setting, the RMS gap between the exact expectation and a ratio of means with the bound $\mathrm{CV}_i+\mathrm{CV}_j$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:conc}, averaged over the 40 pairs. The bound holds for every pair, and the logarithms of the per-pair gap and bound have correlation 0.90. The bound is loose: in most settings it is one to three orders of magnitude above the gap. For a single Gaussian it decreases with dimension, to 0.06--0.12 at $D=1000$. For the mixtures with unequal norms it stays between 1.6 and 3.1 for the global ratio at every dimension, while the per-component bound falls to 0.013--0.078 at $D=1000$. For the embedded two-dimensional law the bound stays near 2 in every dimension: the low effective dimension that it signals is where PN-Taylor errs, although the ratio of means remains accurate there.

